The Competent Authority shall, in conjunction with the relevant agencies, formulate a development framework (the “Unmanned Systems Industry Development Framework”) every four (4) years and review and revise it every two (2) years.
The Framework referred to in the preceding paragraph shall specify the following:
1. industrial development policies and key performance indicators;
2. targets for self-reliance in critical technologies and for domestic content;
3. strategies for developing Unmanned Systems applications;
4. strategies for promoting Trusted Supply Chains;
5. regulatory adjustments and interagency division of responsibilities;
6. domestic market, international market, and export targets;
7. talent development and industry-academia cooperation strategies; and
8. funding requirements, funding source planning, and expected benefits.
The Unmanned Systems Industry Development Framework referred to in Paragraph 1 shall be reviewed and approved by the Special Committee on National Unmanned Systems Industry Development Strategy and approved by the Executive Yuan before being submitted to the Legislative Yuan for recordation.
The Competent Authority shall submit to the Legislative Yuan an annual progress report on Unmanned Systems covering the enhancement of national defense capabilities, industrial development, technological innovation, and economic benefits.

The MOEA, in conjunction with the MND, shall, by March 31 of each year, submit an implementation report to the Executive Yuan and the Legislative Yuan on the development of national defense technologies related to Unmanned Systems during the preceding year. The report shall include:
1. progress toward mass-production targets for each category of Unmanned Systems;
2. progress toward Domestic Content Ratio targets;
3. issuance and revocation of Trusted Supply Chain certifications; and
4. procurement implementation, material deficiencies, and corrective measures.

The MOEA shall support the export of Taiwan-made Unmanned Systems and associated equipment in the following ways:
1. international market forecasting and development;
2. collection and compilation of international application data for reference by government agencies;
3. assistance to industry alliances in pursuing international market opportunities;
4. necessary tax, financing, and investment assistance in accordance with the Statute for Industrial Innovation and other applicable laws and regulations;
5. mapping of common non-military procurement requirements among public-sector entities;
6. grants or subsidies;
7. capital or technology investment or licensing;
8. priority procurement; and
9. financing and preferential interest rates.
When mapping non-military procurement requirements under Subparagraph 5 of the preceding paragraph, public-sector entities shall specify the products, systems and related support systems to be procured, their specific names, quantities, budget amounts, life-cycle sustainment costs, and planned years of implementation.
The regulations governing the timing, eligible recipients, conditions, criteria, and other relevant matters concerning the incentives under Subparagraphs 6 through 9 of Paragraph 1 shall be prescribed by the Competent Authority in consultation with relevant agencies.
The MOEA shall, in conjunction with the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MOFA), the authority in charge of international trade, the authority in charge of standards and inspection, and other relevant agencies, assist the Unmanned Systems industry in keeping abreast of international standards, export controls, end-use requirements, product safety, cybersecurity certification, and market-access requirements.

The MOEA shall establish an Unmanned Systems industry database and periodically collect, compile, and publish the following information:
1. enterprises in the industry value chain, product types, and primary application areas;
2. capabilities and capacity in critical components, system integration, testing and verification, and maintenance services;
3. self-reliance development of critical technologies, technology readiness levels, mass production readiness levels, and verification capabilities;
4. cybersecurity verification, Trusted Supply Chain inspections, international certifications, and alignment with international standards;
5. testing sites, testing corridors, flight-test cases, and relevant safety statistics;
6. types of Unmanned Systems used for public purposes and the status of related safety management;
7. common specifications, common supply contracts, procurement oversight, and maintenance services for Unmanned Systems;
8. talent supply and demand, training, and certification; and
9. export markets, international cooperation, and interoperability standards.
Where information under the preceding paragraph involves national classified information, trade secrets, personal data, cybersecurity, or the security of law enforcement, such information shall be kept confidential or published only after de-identification in accordance with applicable laws.
